{
  "concept": "A digital signature lets Alice sign a message with her secret key S_A and lets anyone verify it with her public key P_A: Bob accepts only if P_A(sigma) equals the message he received.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Heading text 'Alice' at upper left of the diagram",
    "Heading text 'Bob' at upper right of the diagram",
    "Long horizontal line spanning the full width at the bottom, labeled 'communication channel' below it",
    "Message label M' at the far left entering the horizontal line",
    "Vertical branch from the left line turning upward with an arrowhead into Alice's box",
    "Shaded rectangular box labeled S_A with the caption 'sign' above it",
    "Output line from S_A labeled sigma = S_A(M') running right, then turning down with an arrowhead onto the channel line",
    "Channel segment label '(M', sigma)' on the horizontal line between Alice and Bob",
    "Vertical branch on Bob's side rising from the channel, labeled sigma, arrowhead into Bob's box",
    "Shaded rectangular box labeled P_A with the caption 'verify' above it",
    "Output line from P_A turning right then down with an arrowhead into the comparison box",
    "Lower continuation of the channel on Bob's side labeled M'",
    "Upward arrow from the M' line into the bottom of the comparison box",
    "Small shaded box containing '=?'",
    "Arrow leaving the comparison box to the right pointing to the word 'accept'",
    "Dynamic value badges on the channel showing the carried M' and sigma contents",
    "Result badge at the comparison box showing 'accept' (green) or 'reject' (red)",
    "Optional tamper marker (red X / 'altered' tag) placed on the channel segment when tampering is on"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "protocolStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through the protocol",
      "states": ["idle", "sign", "transmit", "verify", "compare"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "The signature is produced from the message before transmission, travels alongside it, and is transformed back at the receiver, so verification is a comparison of two independently obtained copies of the same message."
    },
    {
      "id": "tamper",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Transmission",
      "options": ["untampered", "tampered message M'", "tampered signature sigma"],
      "default": "untampered",
      "teaches": "Verification succeeds only when the received message exactly matches the public-key transform of the received signature; altering either half breaks the equality and Bob rejects."
    },
    {
      "id": "branchHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Why the sigma branch must pass through P_A while the M' branch goes straight to the comparator: only the signature needs the public-key transform to be turned back into a message."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The message enters the channel",
      "narration": "Alice's message M' arrives at the left of the communication channel. Nothing is signed yet — the diagram is idle.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "idle", "tamper": "untampered" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Alice signs",
      "narration": "A branch of M' rises into the shaded box S_A, Alice's secret signing transform. Its output sigma = S_A(M') lights up along the top wire.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "sign", "tamper": "untampered" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The pair travels",
      "narration": "Sigma drops back onto the channel, which from this junction carries the pair (M', sigma). Both halves travel in the clear toward Bob.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "transmit", "tamper": "untampered" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bob verifies with the public key",
      "narration": "At Bob's side the pair splits: sigma branches up into P_A, Alice's public verification transform, which recovers a candidate message from the signature.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "verify", "tamper": "untampered" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare and accept",
      "narration": "The =? box receives P_A(sigma) from above and the received M' from below. They match, so the arrow to 'accept' fires.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "compare", "tamper": "untampered" }
    },
    {
      "title": "An adversary edits the message",
      "narration": "Flip the channel to tampered: the message on the wire is changed but the signature is not. P_A(sigma) still yields Alice's original message, the two inputs disagree, and the =? box turns to reject.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "compare", "tamper": "tampered message M'" }
    },
    {
      "title": "An adversary forges a signature",
      "narration": "Now the signature is altered instead. Without S_A the forger cannot make P_A(sigma) land on the message, so the comparison fails again — this is what makes the scheme unforgeable.",
      "state": { "protocolStep": "compare", "tamper": "tampered signature sigma" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Verification is not a secret operation — it is a public transform followed by an equality test: Bob accepts precisely when P_A(sigma) reproduces the message he received, and only someone holding S_A can make that happen.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Clean black-line schematic on white, textbook style. Top row: serif headings 'Alice' on the left third and 'Bob' on the right third. A single long horizontal line spans the bottom with the caption 'communication channel' centered beneath it. Far left: italic M' feeding the line; a short vertical riser with an arrowhead goes up into a light-gray shaded rectangle labeled italic S_A, with 'sign' in plain text above the box. From S_A a wire runs right under the label 'sigma = S_A(M')', then turns down with an arrowhead onto the channel; the channel segment to the right of that junction is labeled '(M', sigma)'. On Bob's side a riser labeled sigma leaves the channel and arrows into a second gray rectangle labeled P_A, captioned 'verify'. Its output goes right then down with an arrowhead into a small gray box containing '=?'. The channel continues under the label M' and sends an upward arrow into the bottom of the =? box. One arrow exits the =? box to the right to the word 'accept'. Highlighting: active path segments in the step sequence glow in a saturated blue with thicker stroke while inactive segments stay thin gray-black; hovered branch brightens end to end. Tampered state marks the affected channel segment in red with a small altered-value badge, and the terminal word switches from a green 'accept' to a red 'reject'. Small monospace value badges (e.g. 'M' = HELLO', 'sigma = 4f2a') ride on the wires to make the comparison concrete."
}

